# Target C-II — every large Horn upper obstruction localizes inside a low-defect dense train scaffold

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only continuation of the Horn poison-forest extraction; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the 15-spaced one-sign strong-TB1 Horn subsystem through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** Horn poison-forest theorem; exact O--O path interval support; global complete-train count; global angular-defect and terminal-variation budgets; high-`t` crossing-neighbor floor; exact train-step metric; superdiameter-thrackle lemma  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_upper_blocker_dense_grid_localization_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The Horn poison-forest theorem says that the 15-spaced finite-swap system has one of three linear obstruction populations. One is a permanent lower family of fixed path bandwidth at most fourteen. The other two are upper near-diameter families:

- a permanent upper family of density at least `gamma/8`; or
- an implication forest of density at least `gamma/2`,

where

\[
\gamma={791\over9{,}600{,}000{,}000}.
\]

Both upper families have a common extra property: every blocker can be assigned injectively to one retained strong-TB1 coordinate which is one endpoint layer of the blocker. For the implication forest use its distinct tail coordinates. For the permanent family, start from the one-chosen-witness-per-forbidden-coordinate construction and, when one upper pair was chosen for both of its retained endpoints, keep one of those two owners. Deduplication then leaves at least `gamma/8` density and no owner is used twice.

This injective ownership plus exact path-interval support is enough to force a growing piece of every upper obstruction into the existing dense near-equilateral train geometry.

Put

\[
\delta_0:={\gamma\over8}
={791\over76{,}800{,}000{,}000},
\qquad
T_*:=3.0605={6121\over2000}.
\tag{UL.1}
\]

Then every non-LOWER Horn branch contains an owned family `E_D` of distinct upper singleton blockers with

\[
|E_D|>(\delta_0-o(1))D.
\tag{UL.2}

Let

\[
L_D=\left\lfloor {D\over\log D}\right\rfloor.
\tag{UL.3}
\]

The conclusion is the following dichotomy.

> **Upper blocker localization theorem.** Along every such branch, for all sufficiently large `D`, either:
>
> **COMMON-CUT:** one selected O--O row `tau_j` is supported by more than
> \[
> \left(c_* -o(1)\right){D\over\log D}
> \]
> distinct upper blockers of one common geometric type, SIGNED or THIN; or
>
> **LOCAL-WINDOW:** one path window containing at most
> \[
> (2+o(1)){D\over\log D}
> \]
> consecutive path regions contains both endpoints of more than
> \[
> \left(c_* -o(1)\right){D\over\log D}
> \]
> distinct upper blockers of one common geometric type.
>
> In either case the supporting row/window has total train defect `o(D)` and therefore carries one common family of
> \[
> (0.6536-o(1))D
> \]
> transverse columns, all at angle `pi/3+o(1)`, together with the usual common `0.01D` circumcenter flanks of density
> \[
> (0.0647027254265\ldots-o(1))D.
> \]
>
> Here
> \[
> \boxed{
> c_*={\delta_0\over6T_*}
> ={791\over1{,}410{,}278{,}400{,}000}
> =5.60882163408\ldots\times10^{-10}.
> }
> \tag{UL.4}

The constant is small because this statement is unconditional across both upper Horn branches and pays for the earlier 15-spacing, poison extraction, interval-length split, rich-location threshold, and SIGNED/THIN split. The point is not the coefficient: the blocker count diverges like `D/log D` while the whole selected scaffold has vanishing normalized train defect.

---

## 1. Owned upper blockers and exact interval support

Use the selected O--O path

\[
R_0\xleftrightarrow{\tau_1}R_1
\xleftrightarrow{\tau_2}\cdots
\xleftrightarrow{\tau_m}R_m.
\tag{UL.5}
\]

Every source vertex lies in one path region. If an upper blocker `e=xy` has endpoint regions

\[
R_a,R_b,
\qquad a<b,
\]

then the phase-free path theorem gives its exact selected-cut support

\[
\boxed{
I(e)=\{a+1,\ldots,b\},
\qquad
\ell(e):=|I(e)|=b-a.
}
\tag{UL.6}

Every member of `E_D` has one assigned **owner** retained coordinate, equal to one of `a,b`, and distinct blockers have distinct owners.

The selected rows themselves are complete stopped trains. The audited global train count gives

\[
\boxed{
m\le T_*D+o(D),\qquad T_*=3.0605.}
\tag{UL.7}

This very loose upper bound is enough for the interval averaging below.

---

## 2. Every upper singleton blocker is SIGNED or THIN

At the owner coordinate `i`, orient the blocker vector `r` from its fixed endpoint toward the endpoint in the translated layer. Its local exact swap is

\[
r\longmapsto r-q_iw_i,
\qquad
0<q_i\le {K\over D}+o(D^{-1})
\tag{UL.8}
\]

for one source-independent constant `K`, while the singleton state reaches the diameter boundary:

\[
|r|<D,
\qquad
|r-q_iw_i|\ge D.
\tag{UL.9}

Write

\[
\xi=D-|r|.
\]

The triangle inequality gives `0<xi<=q_i`. Split at the sharp superdiameter scale used in the common-super-swap theorem.

### SIGNED

If

\[
\xi\ge {1\over4D},
\tag{UL.10}
\]

then expansion of (UL.9), together with `q_i<=K/D+o(D^-1)`, gives

\[
\boxed{
r\cdot w_i
\le-\left({1\over4K}-o(1)\right)D.}
\tag{UL.11}

The common strong-TB1 axis satisfies `|w_i-w_*|=O(D^-1)`, so

\[
\boxed{
r\cdot w_*
\le-\left({1\over4K}-o(1)\right)D.}
\tag{UL.12}

### THIN

If instead

\[
0<\xi<{1\over4D},
\tag{UL.13}
\]

then

\[
|r|>D-{1\over4D}>\sqrt{D^2-1}
\tag{UL.14}
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`. Hence every THIN blocker belongs to the existing straight-line superdiameter geometric thrackle. In particular its endpoints are hull vertices, and any two vertex-disjoint THIN blockers cross properly.

Thus every blocker in `E_D` has one of exactly two useful upper geometries.

---

## 3. Split by path-support length

Put `L=L_D` from (UL.3) and divide `E_D` into

\[
E_D^{long}=\{e:\ell(e)\ge L\},
\qquad
E_D^{short}=\{e:\ell(e)<L\}.
\tag{UL.15}
\]

One class has size at least

\[
>{\delta_0\over2}D-o(D).
\tag{UL.16}

The two cases use different averaging arguments.

---

## 4. Long supports force a blocker-rich low-defect common cut

Assume

\[
|E_D^{long}|>{\delta_0\over2}D-o(D).
\tag{UL.17}

For each selected cut `tau_j`, let

\[
h_j=|\{e\in E_D^{long}:j\in I(e)\}|.
\tag{UL.18}

Summing cut incidences gives

\[
\sum_{j=1}^m h_j
=\sum_{e\in E_D^{long}}\ell(e)
>\left({\delta_0\over2}-o(1)\right)DL.
\tag{UL.19}

Set the rich-cut threshold

\[
\boxed{
\vartheta={\delta_0\over3T_*}.}
\tag{UL.20}

Call a cut rich when

\[
h_j\ge\vartheta L.
\tag{UL.21}

The total incidence carried by poor cuts is at most

\[
m\vartheta L
\le\left({\delta_0\over3}+o(1)\right)DL.
\tag{UL.22}

Hence rich cuts carry total incidence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j\ rich}h_j
>\left({\delta_0\over6}-o(1)\right)DL.}
\tag{UL.23}

Let

\[
F_j=\sum_{b\in\Gamma_j}\delta_{jb}
\tag{UL.24}

be the full angular-defect sum on row `tau_j`. The canonical global phase budget is

\[
\sum_jF_j=O(D).
\tag{UL.25}

Weighted averaging over the rich cuts in (UL.23) gives one rich `j` with

\[
{F_j\over h_j}=O(L^{-1}).
\tag{UL.26}

Because blockers have distinct owners,

\[
h_j\le |E_D|\le m+1=O(D),
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{F_j=O(D/L)=O(\log D)=o(D).}
\tag{UL.27}

The rich row supports at least

\[
h_j\ge\vartheta L
\tag{UL.28}

upper blockers. Split them into SIGNED and THIN. One type has size at least

\[
{\vartheta L\over2}
=\left({\delta_0\over6T_*}-o(1)\right){D\over\log D}
=(c_*-o(1)){D\over\log D}.
\tag{UL.29}

This is the COMMON-CUT alternative.

### Dense train scaffold on the rich row

Every selected high-`t` row has

\[
|\Gamma_j|>(0.6536-o(1))D.
\tag{UL.30}

Because `F_j=o(D)`, choose `epsilon_D->0` slowly enough that

\[
{F_j\over\epsilon_D}=o(D).
\]

Delete every column with angular defect greater than `epsilon_D`. The retained family has

\[
(0.6536-o(1))D
\]

columns and every crossing angle is

\[
{\pi\over3}+o(1).
\]

The exact train-step metric and source circumdisk bound then give the standard `mu=0.01` initial and terminal flank blocks, each of density

\[
0.6536-{1.02\over\sqrt3}
=0.0647027254265\ldots .
\tag{UL.31}

Every retained blocker in (UL.29) has endpoints on opposite shores of this same selected row by exact support (UL.6).

---

## 5. Short supports force a blocker-rich low-defect window

Now assume

\[
|E_D^{short}|>{\delta_0\over2}D-o(D).
\tag{UL.32}

Partition the path-region indices into consecutive blocks of `L` indices,

\[
B_r=\{rL,\ldots,(r+1)L-1\},
\]

truncated at the path ends. For a short blocker with endpoint indices `a<b`, assign it to the block containing `a`.

Because

\[
b-a<L,
\]

both endpoints lie in the two-block window

\[
W_r:=B_r\cup B_{r+1}.
\tag{UL.33}

Let `e_r` be the number of short blockers assigned to `W_r`. There are at most

\[
{T_*D\over L}+o(D/L)
\tag{UL.34}

nonempty candidate windows.

Call `W_r` rich if

\[
e_r\ge\vartheta L
\tag{UL.35}

with the same `vartheta` as (UL.20). The total number of blockers in poor windows is at most

\[
\left({T_*D\over L}+o(D/L)\right)\vartheta L
\le\left({\delta_0\over3}+o(1)\right)D.
\tag{UL.36}

Thus rich windows contain altogether more than

\[
\boxed{
\left({\delta_0\over6}-o(1)\right)D}
\tag{UL.37}

short blockers.

### Full train-defect cost of a window

For a window `W_r`, let `C_r` be

- the sum of all row angular defects `F_j` on selected rows between the first and last region of `W_r`; plus
- the sum of all adjacent terminal-variation costs `L_j` between those rows.

The canonical budgets give

\[
\sum_jF_j=O(D),
\qquad
\sum_jL_j=O(D).
\tag{UL.38}

Each row/interface belongs to at most two two-block windows, so

\[
\boxed{\sum_r C_r=O(D).}
\tag{UL.39}

Weighted averaging over the rich windows in (UL.37) gives one rich `W_r` with

\[
{C_r\over e_r}=O(1).
\tag{UL.40}

Injective blocker ownership is now essential. Every blocker assigned to `W_r` has its owner at one of its endpoints, hence inside `W_r`, and distinct blockers have distinct owners. A two-block window contains at most

\[
2L+O(1)
\]

path-region indices. Therefore

\[
\boxed{e_r\le2L+O(1).}
\tag{UL.41}

Combining (UL.40)--(UL.41),

\[
\boxed{C_r=O(L)=O(D/\log D)=o(D).}
\tag{UL.42}

The rich window contains at least `vartheta L` blockers. Splitting SIGNED versus THIN leaves one type of size

\[
>(c_*-o(1)){D\over\log D}.
\tag{UL.43}

All of their endpoints lie in a path window of at most

\[
2L+O(1)=(2+o(1)){D\over\log D}
\]

regions. This is the LOCAL-WINDOW alternative.

### One dense common train ladder across the whole window

For each selected row `tau_j` in the window let `Gamma_j` be its crossing-train family. The adjacent terminal-topology theorem gives

\[
|\Gamma_j\triangle\Gamma_{j+1}|\le L_j.
\tag{UL.44}

The `L`-part of (UL.42) is `o(D)`. Starting from one row and intersecting successively therefore leaves

\[
\boxed{
\left|\bigcap_{j\in W_r}\Gamma_j\right|
>(0.6536-o(1))D.}
\tag{UL.45}

The `F`-part of (UL.42) is also `o(D)`. For each common column `b`, sum its angular defects over all rows of the window. Delete the columns whose summed defect exceeds a threshold `epsilon_D->0` chosen so slowly that the number deleted is `o(D)`. Then one common family of

\[
(0.6536-o(1))D
\]

columns remains, and **every** row-column crossing in the whole growing window has angle

\[
{\pi\over3}+o(1).
\tag{UL.46}

The columns are pairwise noncrossing and occur in one common order on every row. The exact train-step metric gives the same two `0.01D` circumcenter flank blocks (UL.31) on every row simultaneously.

Thus the short upper obstruction is confined to a sublinear-width, vanishing-defect, dense near-equilateral train ladder.

---

## 6. Unconditional Horn frontier after localization

Combine this theorem with the Horn poison-forest extraction. Along the one-sign strong-TB1 Horn branch, one of the following must occur:

```text
LOWER:
    > (gamma/4-o(1)) D permanent near-unit blockers,
    each with path bandwidth <= 14;

or UPPER / COMMON-CUT:
    > (c_*-o(1)) D/log D blockers of one type (SIGNED or THIN)
    all support one selected row having (0.6536-o(1))D
    pi/3+o(1) columns and common circumcenter flanks;

or UPPER / LOCAL-WINDOW:
    > (c_*-o(1)) D/log D blockers of one type (SIGNED or THIN)
    have both endpoints inside <=(2+o(1))D/log D path regions,
    and that entire window shares (0.6536-o(1))D
    pi/3+o(1) columns and common circumcenter flanks.
```

For THIN, every blocker is additionally a superdiameter thrackle chord. For SIGNED, every blocker has a fixed macroscopic negative projection on the common TB1 transverse axis.

This closes the synchronization gap for the upper Horn obstruction: a large implication/permanent-upper system can no longer remain spread arbitrarily through high-defect parts of the O--O path. It contains a growing ordered subfamily inside a train scaffold whose normalized defect tends to zero.

C-II remains open. The remaining upper task is now geometric inside the two displayed scaffolds. The lower task is separate and fixed-bandwidth: eliminate a linear family of permanent near-unit blockers supported on at most fourteen path cuts.
